one day after receiving advice from  australia's solicitor general prime  minister anthony albanese unveiled his  report  it's found that his predecessor's  decision to appoint himself covertly to  five ministerial roles was legal but  failing to inform his cabinet the  parliament the public and for ministers  was inconsistent with how the country  should be run  the advice so  is i think a very clear  clear  criticism  and critique  of the implications that are there for  our democratic system of government  during the pandemic scott morrison swore  himself in as joint minister for health  home affairs finance treasury and  resources  the governor general oversaw the  appointment but he too didn't inform  parliament that was cleared by the  nation's top lawyer albanese says a  wider inquiry is needed  the inquiry will need to examine what  happened and how it happened  it will also need to examine what the  implications are  for what occurred  over whether there are any uh legal  issues that are raised  constitutional lawyers agree and say  that parliament needs to be informed of  all ministerial appointments if  democracy is to be upheld if we have  secret ministers as it seems we had uh  that's an affront to those principles  and to those norms that  frustrates uh the ability and prevents  parliament holding ministers accountable  if you don't know who the ministers are  and which ministers are responsible for  what uh parliament can't that process of  ministerial accountability department  doesn't work prime minister albanese has  flagged the loophole that allows  ministry appointments to be made without  informing the public and has asked his  department to make that a priority  as for scott morrison the former prime  minister said it was in the national  interest for him to take on the extra  portfolios during the pandemic an  extraordinary and unprecedented time  so far he's refused to resign sarah  clark al jazeera brisbane australia
 

